BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 676.4 Td (in programming languages to implement transactional memory.) Tj ET
BT /F2 12.5 Tf 56 629.8 Td (Description) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 579.2 Td (Without concurrency control, if someone is reading from a database at the same time as someone) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 565.7 Td (else is writing to it, it is possible that the reader will see a half-written or inconsistent) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 552.2 Td (piece of data. For instance, when making a wire transfer between two bank accounts if a reader) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 538.7 Td (reads the balance at the bank when the money has been withdrawn from the original account and) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 525.2 Td (before it was deposited in the destination account, it would seem that money has disappeared) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 511.7 Td (from the bank. Isolation is the property that provides guarantees in the concurrent accesses to) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 498.2 Td (data. Isolation is implemented by means of a concurrency control protocol. The simplest way is) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 484.7 Td (to make all readers wait until the writer is done, which is known as a read-write lock. Locks) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 471.2 Td (are known to create contention especially between long read transactions and update) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 457.7 Td (transactions. MVCC aims at solving the problem by keeping multiple copies of each data item. In) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 444.2 Td (this way, each user connected to the database sees a snapshot of the database at a particular) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 430.7 Td (instant in time. Any changes made by a writer will not be seen by other users of the database) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 417.2 Td (until the changes have been completed \(or, in database terms: until the transaction has been) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 403.7 Td (committed.\)) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 360.5 Td (When an MVCC database needs to update a piece of data, it will not overwrite the original data) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 347.0 Td (item with new data, but instead creates a newer version of the data item. Thus there are) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 333.5 Td (multiple versions stored. The version that each transaction sees depends on the isolation level) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 320.0 Td (implemented. The most common isolation level implemented with MVCC is snapshot isolation. With) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 306.5 Td (snapshot isolation, a transaction observes a state of the data as of when the transaction) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 293.0 Td (started.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 249.8 Td (MVCC provides point-in-time consistent views. Read transactions under MVCC typically use a) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 236.3 Td (timestamp or transaction ID to determine what state of the DB to read, and read these versions) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 222.8 Td (of the data. Read and write transactions are thus isolated from each other without any need for) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 209.3 Td (locking. However, despite locks being unnecessary, they are used by some MVCC databases such as) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 195.8 Td (Oracle. Writes create a newer version, while concurrent reads access an older version.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 152.6 Td (MVCC introduces the challenge of how to remove versions that become obsolete and will never be) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 139.1 Td (read. In some cases, a process to periodically sweep through and delete the obsolete versions) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 125.6 Td (is implemented. This is often a stop-the-world process that traverses a whole table and) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 112.1 Td (rewrites it with the last version of each data item. PostgreSQL can use this approach with its) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 98.6 Td (VACUUM FREEZE process. Other databases split the storage blocks into two parts: the data part) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 85.1 Td (and an undo log. The data part always keeps the last committed version. The undo log enables) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 71.6 Td (the recreation of older versions of data. The main inherent limitation of this latter approach) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.0 Tf 56 58.1 Td (is that when there are update-intensive workloads, the undo log part runs out of space and then) Tj ET
